 "name": "task-4fbd50f1-diagonal-ray-extension",
 "old_string": "This task-specific note is based on correct recurring instances plus demonstrations; treat it as a strong prior, re-derive directions and eligible motifs from every fresh grid, and validate all dimensions and changed coordinates.",
 "new_string": "### Additional recurring instance evidence (task-4fbd50f1)\nA 7x18 test with background 9 had 5s at `(3,2)` and `(4,1)`, plus a singleton 4 at `(4,0)` and 1s at `(5,1),(6,2)`. The correct output added only 5s at `(2,3),(1,4),(0,5)`, extending the diagonal pair upward-right to the boundary. The vertical 1 pair does not qualify, and the singleton 4 is unchanged. This was confirmed after two wrong submissions; the first incorrectly extended 5 into an unrelated diagonal and generated spurious rays from nearby cells, while the second left the grid unchanged. Use the clear repeated-color diagonal pair as the ray cue; do not promote nearby non-diagonal pairs or invent extra extensions.\n\nAnother demonstration (25x15, background 6) had 1s at `(16,1),(17,2)` and extended them down-right through `(18,3)` to `(24,9)`, without changing the nearby 5s or singleton 9. Thus extension direction is the continuation of the diagonal alignment away from the motif, to the grid boundary.\n\nThis task-specific note is based on correct recurring instances plus demonstrations; treat it as a strong prior, re-derive directions and eligible motifs from every fresh grid, and validate all dimensions and changed coordinates."
}
